{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 33, :comments ["<b>And the Egyptians pressured the people.</b> After they were struck with ten plagues, they were forced to send them off against their will — and furthermore, Israel took all their wealth from them; this is what it says: 'and they despoiled the Egyptians.' Thus the Rabbis of blessed memory expounded in the Midrash on Mishlei: What is written (Proverbs 27:17) ״ברזל בברזל יחד ואיש יחד פני רעהו״ — 'Iron sharpens iron, and a man sharpens the face of his fellow'? ״ברזל בברזל יחד״ — this is Moses the righteous and Pharaoh the wicked, who would lock horns with one another in words. When Moses entered before Pharaoh, he said to him: 'Who sent you?' He said to him: 'The Elohim of the Hebrews sent me to you.' He said to him: 'And what did He say to you?' He said to him: ״שלח את עמי ויעבדוני״ — 'Send out My people that they may serve Me.' He said to him: 'Is there a god in the world whom I do not recognize? As your life, every deity in the world has sent me letters, but this god that you mention has never sent me a letter.' Since he did not find the name of the Holy One, blessed be He, he said to him: 'Did I not say to you, ״לא ידעתי את ה׳״ — \"I do not know YHWH\"?' Immediately he sent and brought all the wise men of Egypt. He said to them: 'Have you ever heard from your fathers the name of the god of these people?' They said to him: 'Thus we have heard — that He is the son of sages, the son of the kings of the East, as it is said (Isaiah 19:11) ״אך אוילים שרי צען חכמי יועצי פרעה וגו׳״ — \"Only fools are the princes of Zoan, the wise counselors of Pharaoh, etc.\"' He said to them: 'Fools of the world — you have called yourselves sages, yet to me [he is but] the son of sages! As your lives, I shall destroy your wisdom from the world, as it is said (ibid. v. 14) ״ואבדה חכמת חכמיו״ — \"And the wisdom of his wise men shall perish.\" You say ״לא ידעתי את ה׳״ — \"I do not know YHWH\" — in the end you will come to know Him. ״וגם את ישראל לא אשלח״ — \"And also Israel I will not send forth\" — in the end you will send them forth against your will.' A parable — what is the matter like? Like a nobleman who said to his servant: 'Go and bring me a fish from the market.' He went and brought him a spoiled fish. He said to him: 'I impose upon you three choices: either eat the fish, or receive a hundred lashes, or pay a hundred mina.' He said to him: 'I will eat the fish.' He did not manage to eat it before he said: 'I will take a hundred lashes.' He received fifty or sixty lashes — he did not manage to finish being lashed before he said: 'I will pay a hundred mina.' It turned out that he ate the spoiled fish and received the lashes and paid the hundred mina. Thus the Holy One, blessed be He, did to Egypt: they were struck, and they sent forth Israel, and Israel took their wealth from them. What caused all this to come upon them? Because they hardened themselves against the Holy One, blessed be He — so too He hardened Himself against them, through Moses, as it is said: ״ברזל בברזל יחד״ — 'Iron with iron together.'"], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:58:22.376947040Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}